In this lesson we'll look at how to graph the inverse of a function using a simple reflection trick. So here's the big idea. If you have a point on the graph of f, say it sits at a and b, swapping the roles of x and y gives you the point b and a. And that swap is exactly what happens when you reflect a point across the line y equals x. So the graph of the inverse is just the graph of the original function, flipped over that diagonal line like a mirror. There are a couple of neat consequences worth noticing. First, intercepts swap places. Wherever f cuts the y-axis at a point, the inverse will cut the x-axis at that same height. Second, if the two graphs happen to cross, they always meet right on the line y equals x, because any point on that line is its own mirror image. Picture folding the page along the diagonal, and the two curves land perfectly on top of each other. Now let's see it in action.